<!-- ============================================================
     CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: DNS-Layer and
     Process-Execution Detection Gap Coverage
     Threat: CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog subgroup, APT-Q-27 /
             Dragon Breath / Miuuti Group); China-nexus cybercrime
             and espionage collective. DigiCert breach April 2026:
             60 stolen code-signing certificates used to sign 27+
             Golden Gh0st RAT and Loader specimens, defeating AV at
             deployment time.

             Golden Gh0st RAT C2 domains:
               uu.goldeyeuu[.]io  (TCP 5188, unencrypted commands)
               api.keensie[.]com  (TCP 5198, encrypted exfiltration)

             Known IOC SHA256:
               81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de

     Gap Context (ESKA-599):
             Rules 104089-104095 (ESKA-595) cover firewall/network-
             layer C2 domain and port detection, Sysmon EventID 3
             (NetworkConnect), and FIM-based file hash matching.

             This file closes three detection gaps:
             1. DNS-LAYER: Detects the DNS query to C2 domains BEFORE
                the network connection is established. Catches Golden
                Gh0st RAT beaconing even when the firewall blocks the
                outbound connection. Covers three DNS log sources:
                  a. Unbound/OPNsense DNS resolver logs (endpoint
                     queried internal recursive resolver)
                  b. Suricata DNS EVE logs (passive network DNS)
                  c. Sysmon EventID 22 (Windows DNS client events)
             2. PROCESS-HASH: Detects process creation and module
                loading events where the SHA256 hash of the launched
                binary or loaded DLL matches the known Golden Gh0st
                RAT specimen hash. Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate)
                and EventID 7 (ImageLoad) both include a Hashes field
                that Wazuh decodes for rule matching. FIM (rule 104095)
                detects the binary at rest; these rules detect runtime
                execution of the same hash.

     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (C2 DNS)
       T1573     - Encrypted Channel (TCP 5198 exfil channel)
       T1553.002 - Code Signing (stolen DigiCert certificates)

     Rule IDs: 104096-104102
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Issue: ESKA-599
     Date: 2026-07-21

     Log source prerequisites:
       Rules 104096-104097 (Unbound DNS):
         OPNsense or standalone Unbound DNS resolver with query
         logging enabled, forwarded to Wazuh. Wazuh decoder must
         assign the unbound,dns group. The data.query field contains
         the queried hostname. Source IP of the querying client is
         in data.srcip (or data.client depending on decoder version).
       Rule 104098 (Sysmon EventID 22):
         Sysmon EventID 22 (DnsQuery) logging enabled in the Sysmon
         configuration with a rule to capture all DNS queries (or
         a targeted rule for the known C2 domains). The Wazuh agent
         must forward the Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational
         channel. The decoded field win.eventdata.queryName holds the
         queried domain; win.eventdata.image holds the querying process.
       Rule 104099 (Suricata DNS EVE):
         Suricata with EVE JSON output enabled and dns logging active.
         Wazuh receives the Suricata JSON via the Suricata integration
         (SID 86601 base rule). The dns.query.rrname field contains
         the queried hostname from passive DNS inspection.
       Rules 104100-104101 (Sysmon EventID 1 and 7):
         Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) and EventID 7 (ImageLoad)
         must be enabled in the Sysmon configuration. Wazuh decodes
         these into SID 61603 (ProcessCreate) and SID 61644 (ImageLoad
         and other Sysmon events). The win.eventdata.hashes field
         contains a comma-separated list of hash values in the format
         SHA256=<hash>,SHA1=<hash>,MD5=<hash>. The PCRE2 pattern
         matches the known Golden Gh0st RAT SHA256 within that string.
       Rule 104102 (ImageLoad, golden ghost DLL):
         Same prerequisites as rule 104101, but fires on EventID 7
         (ImageLoad), which records every module (DLL/EXE) loaded by
         any process. Useful when Golden Gh0st RAT is injected as a
         reflective DLL rather than executed as a standalone process
         (in which case EventID 1 may not fire for the RAT itself).

     Severity tiers:
       14 - DNS query for known C2 domain (single event, high conf)
       15 - Repeated DNS queries = active RAT beaconing (2+/120 s)
       14 - Suricata passive DNS hit for known C2 domain
       15 - Sysmon EventID 1: process created with Golden Gh0st hash
       14 - Sysmon EventID 7: module loaded with Golden Gh0st hash

     References:
       Expel (2026-07-20): https://expel.com/blog/introducing-cylindricalcanine/
       The Hacker News (2026-07):
         https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/goldeneyedog-subgroup-linked-to.html
       MITRE ATT&CK Dragon Breath G1071:
         https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1071/
       MITRE ATT&CK T1071.001:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/001/
       MITRE ATT&CK T1553.002:
         https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1553/002/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,command_and_control,windows,linux,macos,cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dragon_breath,china_nexus,dns,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104096: DNS-layer C2 domain query via Unbound resolver
       Unbound/OPNsense DNS resolver logs - group unbound,dns
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       This rule fires when an endpoint's DNS resolver receives a
       query for either known Golden Gh0st RAT C2 domain. Unlike
       firewall rules 104089-104090 (which require the TCP connection
       to reach the network sensor), this rule fires at the DNS lookup
       stage: even when the outbound connection is blocked by a
       firewall ACL, the DNS query must still be resolved first and
       is visible in Unbound/OPNsense resolver logs. This provides
       an earlier detection signal and catches infected endpoints
       behind strict egress filtering.

       goldeyeuu.io: Golden Gh0st RAT command channel domain
                     (TCP 5188, unencrypted, attacker sends tasks)
       keensie.com:  Golden Gh0st RAT exfil channel domain
                     (TCP 5198, encrypted, credentials/screenshots)

       False positives: none. These are confirmed CylindricalCanine
       C2 domains with no known legitimate use. Any DNS query for
       either domain indicates Golden Gh0st RAT activity. Isolate
       the querying host and initiate incident response immediately.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104096" level="14">
    <if_group>unbound,dns,</if_group>
    <field name="data.query" type="pcre2">(?i)(uu\.goldeyeuu\.io|api\.keensie\.com)</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: DNS query for confirmed C2 domain from $(agent.name) -- endpoint queried resolver for uu.goldeyeuu[.]io (Golden Gh0st command channel, TCP 5188) or api.keensie[.]com (encrypted exfil, TCP 5198); DNS-layer detection fires even when firewall blocks the outbound connection; CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) China-nexus espionage group signed RAT with 60 stolen DigiCert certificates to bypass AV; isolate querying host immediately (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1553.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dns,ioc_c2,ioc_domain,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104097: DNS beaconing via Unbound (active RAT confirmed)
       Frequency rule on 104096 - 2+ queries in 120 s, same source
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)
              T1573 (Encrypted Channel)

       Golden Gh0st RAT performs periodic DNS lookups for its C2
       domains as part of its heartbeat/reconnect logic. Two or
       more DNS queries for either C2 domain from the same client
       IP within 120 seconds confirms the RAT is running and
       performing regular C2 check-ins via DNS resolution.

       This frequency pattern distinguishes a live implant with an
       active reconnect loop from a one-time failed resolution.
       Given China-nexus attribution (CylindricalCanine/Dragon
       Breath/APT-Q-27), the endpoint is likely under active
       adversary control. Escalate to CISO and IR.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104097" level="15" frequency="2" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104096</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT CRITICAL: active C2 DNS beaconing confirmed from $(agent.name) -- 2+ DNS queries to Golden Gh0st C2 domains (uu.goldeyeuu[.]io or api.keensie[.]com) within 120 seconds from the same host; active Golden Gh0st RAT implant is performing regular DNS-based C2 check-ins; China-nexus CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) espionage; CRITICAL: isolate host NOW, capture memory, escalate to IR (T1071.001, T1573)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1573</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dns,beaconing,active_rat,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104098: Sysmon EventID 22 (Windows DNS client) C2 query
       Sysmon EventID 22 (DnsQuery) - Wazuh SID 61644
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       Sysmon EventID 22 (DnsQuery) captures DNS queries made by
       every Windows process and records the querying process name,
       image path, and the resolved domain. This provides endpoint-
       side visibility that is independent of the network/resolver
       layer: even if internal DNS resolution bypasses the monitored
       recursive resolver, or if DNS-over-HTTPS is in use, Sysmon
       EventID 22 still records the API-level query. It also provides
       process attribution (which binary triggered the DNS lookup)
       that Unbound/Suricata DNS logs do not capture.

       Golden Gh0st RAT queries its C2 domains to resolve the current
       C2 IP before initiating WebSocket connections on TCP 5188/5198.
       The querying process image path is preserved in the event and
       surfaced in the alert description for immediate triage.

       Requires Sysmon EventID 22 (DnsQuery) enabled in the Sysmon
       configuration. In many default Sysmon configs EventID 22 is
       disabled; confirm it is active with a rule like:
         <EventFiltering><DnsQuery onmatch="exclude"/></EventFiltering>
       to log all DNS queries. A scoped include rule targeting the
       known C2 domains reduces noise while preserving coverage.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104098" level="14">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^22$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.queryName" type="pcre2">(?i)(uu\.goldeyeuu\.io|api\.keensie\.com)</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: Windows DNS query for confirmed C2 domain by $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) -- Sysmon EventID 22 captured a DNS lookup for uu.goldeyeuu[.]io (command channel) or api.keensie[.]com (encrypted exfil); process-level attribution via Sysmon provides the querying binary path; Golden Gh0st RAT resolves C2 domain before establishing WebSocket connection on TCP 5188 or 5198; C2 domains attributed to CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) China-nexus espionage; isolate host immediately (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1553.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dns,windows,sysmon,ioc_c2,ioc_domain,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104099: Suricata passive DNS: C2 domain query detected
       Suricata EVE DNS log - Wazuh SID 86601
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       Suricata with EVE JSON output passively inspects DNS query
       traffic on the wire (UDP/TCP port 53 and DoT port 853) and
       logs the queried domain in dns.query.rrname. This provides
       network-layer DNS visibility that captures queries from all
       hosts on the monitored segment simultaneously, without
       requiring agent deployment or Sysmon configuration on each
       endpoint. Particularly valuable for detecting Golden Gh0st
       RAT on non-Windows hosts (Linux, embedded, or IoT devices
       that CylindricalCanine may pivot through after initial
       Windows compromise) or on hosts where Wazuh agents are not
       installed.

       A DNS query for either C2 domain observed on the wire is a
       confirmed indicator of a Golden Gh0st RAT implant performing
       a C2 check-in or re-resolution. Correlate with firewall
       rules 104089-104090 on the same source IP to determine
       whether the subsequent TCP connection succeeded or was blocked.

       False positives: none. These domains are exclusively
       CylindricalCanine C2 infrastructure.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104099" level="14">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="dns.query.rrname" type="pcre2">(?i)(uu\.goldeyeuu\.io|api\.keensie\.com)</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: Suricata passive DNS: query for confirmed C2 domain detected from $(agent.name) -- DNS lookup for uu.goldeyeuu[.]io (unencrypted command channel, TCP 5188) or api.keensie[.]com (encrypted exfil channel, TCP 5198) observed on the wire; both domains are confirmed CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) infrastructure; correlate with firewall rules 104089-104090 to determine whether the subsequent connection succeeded; isolate the source host and initiate IR (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dns,suricata,ioc_c2,ioc_domain,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104100: Sysmon EventID 1 process creation with known hash
       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603
       MITRE: T1553.002 (Code Signing - stolen cert defense evasion)

       Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) records the SHA256 hash of
       every executed binary in the win.eventdata.hashes field as a
       comma-separated list (e.g. SHA256=<hash>,SHA1=<hash>,MD5=<hash>).
       This rule fires when a process is created whose hash matches
       the known Golden Gh0st RAT specimen:
         81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de

       Unlike FIM rule 104095 (which detects the binary at rest on
       disk when it is written or modified), this rule detects runtime
       execution. A file can arrive on disk without being detected if
       FIM is not monitoring that specific path; execution via Sysmon
       EventID 1 provides a safety net that catches the RAT when it
       actually runs, regardless of where it was stored.

       Golden Gh0st RAT binaries are signed with stolen DigiCert
       code-signing certificates (CylindricalCanine April 2026 breach).
       The binary will appear legitimately signed. Do not dismiss this
       alert because the binary has a valid digital signature. The
       hash is what uniquely identifies the malware specimen.

       False positives: none. The SHA256 hash uniquely identifies
       this confirmed Golden Gh0st RAT specimen.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104100" level="15">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^1$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.hashes" type="pcre2">(?i)81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT CRITICAL: known malicious binary executed on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user) -- Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) recorded execution of process with SHA256 hash 81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de, a confirmed Golden Gh0st RAT specimen signed with a DigiCert certificate stolen in the CylindricalCanine breach; binary path: $(win.eventdata.image); the file appears legitimately signed and AV may have permitted execution; CRITICAL: isolate host NOW, capture memory, quarantine process, escalate to IR (T1553.002, T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1553.002</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,windows,sysmon,process_execution,ioc_hash,code_signing,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104101: Sysmon EventID 7 image load with known RAT hash
       Sysmon EventID 7 (ImageLoad) - Wazuh SID 61644
       MITRE: T1553.002 (Code Signing - stolen cert defense evasion)

       Sysmon EventID 7 (ImageLoad) fires when a module (DLL or
       executable) is loaded into a process's address space. Like
       EventID 1, it includes a Hashes field containing the SHA256
       of the loaded module. This rule detects the known Golden Gh0st
       RAT hash when it is loaded as a DLL into another process, which
       covers the reflective DLL injection technique where an attacker
       injects the RAT into a running host process (e.g. explorer.exe,
       svchost.exe) instead of launching it as a standalone executable.

       In that scenario Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) would fire
       for the injecting loader but NOT for the injected RAT DLL.
       EventID 7 catches the DLL load event regardless of whether the
       module was executed directly, loaded via LoadLibrary, or
       injected reflectively.

       CylindricalCanine signed Golden Gh0st RAT with stolen DigiCert
       certificates; the loaded module will appear legitimately signed.
       Verify the signing certificate serial number if time permits;
       however the hash match is definitive; do not wait for cert
       verification before initiating incident response.

       Requires Sysmon EventID 7 (ImageLoad) enabled in the Sysmon
       configuration. Due to high event volume, many configs exclude
       EventID 7 or apply strict filters. At minimum, configure a
       targeted Sysmon ImageLoad rule to capture events from
       non-standard or unsigned DLLs loaded into sensitive processes.

       False positives: none. This is a confirmed malicious file hash.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104101" level="14">
    <if_sid>61644</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^7$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.hashes" type="pcre2">(?i)81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de</field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT: confirmed RAT module loaded into process $(win.eventdata.image) on $(win.eventdata.computer) -- Sysmon EventID 7 (ImageLoad) detected a DLL or executable with SHA256 hash 81e276aaa3eb9b3f595663c316b3c6414cc3dde5e6cc3a82856b7276acabb7de loaded into a running process; this detects reflective DLL injection of Golden Gh0st RAT where no separate process creation event fires; CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) uses stolen DigiCert certs so the module appears signed; quarantine the host and capture memory before removing (T1553.002, T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1553.002</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,windows,sysmon,dll_load,ioc_hash,code_signing,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 104102: Sysmon EventID 22 DNS beaconing (2+/120s)
       Frequency rule on 104098 - repeated Windows DNS client queries
       MITRE: T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols)

       Golden Gh0st RAT re-resolves its C2 domains at regular
       intervals as part of its reconnect heartbeat. Two or more
       Windows DNS client queries (Sysmon EventID 22) for either
       C2 domain from the same host within 120 seconds confirms that
       an active Golden Gh0st RAT implant is running on the Windows
       endpoint and performing routine DNS-based check-ins.

       This frequency rule on EventID 22 is the endpoint-layer
       analogue of rule 104097 (Unbound DNS beaconing). Both rules
       may fire simultaneously for an infected Windows host where
       both Sysmon and Unbound resolver logging are active. Multiple
       rule triggers from the same host increase confidence and
       provide stronger evidence for host isolation decisions.

       The querying process name captured by Sysmon EventID 22
       (win.eventdata.image) will be the Golden Gh0st RAT binary or
       its loader process. Correlate the image path with FIM rule
       104095 and process creation rule 104100 to confirm.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104102" level="15" frequency="2" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104098</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.eventdata.computer</same_field>
    <description>CylindricalCanine Golden Gh0st RAT CRITICAL: active C2 DNS beaconing confirmed via Sysmon EventID 22 on $(win.eventdata.computer) -- 2+ Windows DNS client queries (Sysmon EventID 22) for Golden Gh0st C2 domains (uu.goldeyeuu[.]io or api.keensie[.]com) within 120 seconds from the same host; active implant is performing regular C2 DNS check-ins; endpoint-level DNS evidence confirms infection; CylindricalCanine (GoldenEyeDog/APT-Q-27/Dragon Breath) espionage; CRITICAL: isolate host NOW, capture memory, escalate to IR (T1071.001, T1573)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1573</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>cylindricalcanine,golden_ghost_rat,dns,windows,sysmon,beaconing,active_rat,china_nexus,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
